```python
panel_model(
    data,
    y,
    x,
    *,
    models=('pooled', 'between', 'fe', 're'),
    time_effects=False,
    cluster=True,
    title=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

The standard panel estimators side by side, and the Hausman test of fixed against random effects.

The units are ``geo_id`` and the periods ``year``; every year with data is used.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name         | Type        | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Default                             |
|--------------|-------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| data         |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` and ``year``, e.g. from ``load`` or ``combine``                                                                                                                                                  | _required_                          |
| y            | str         | The outcome column                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | _required_                          |
| x            |             | The regressors: a column or a list of columns                                                                                                                                                                                             | _required_                          |
| models       |             | The estimators to compare, in order: ``"pooled"`` (least squares on all rows), ``"between"`` (on the units' averages over the years), ``"fe"`` (fixed effects: each unit's deviations from its own average) and ``"re"`` (random effects) | `('pooled', 'between', 'fe', 're')` |
| time_effects | bool        | Add year effects (to every estimator but ``"between"``)                                                                                                                                                                                   | `False`                             |
| cluster      | bool        | Standard errors clustered by unit; ``False``: heteroskedasticity-robust. The between estimator, with one row per unit, is always heteroskedasticity-robust                                                                                | `True`                              |
| title        | str \| None | The title of the table and of the figure                                                                                                                                                                                                  | `None`                              |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type             | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
|--------|------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | PanelModelResult | A result with ``.gt`` (the comparison table), ``.fig`` (the coefficients with 95% confidence intervals across estimators), ``.df`` (tidy: ``model``, ``term``, ``estimate``, ``std_error``, ``statistic``, ``p_value``, ``conf_low``, ``conf_high``), ``.summary`` (one row per model: ``n``, ``units``, ``years``, ``r2``, ``r2_within``, ``r2_between``, ``r2_overall``), ``.models`` (the fitted linearmodels results, keyed by estimator), ``.hausman`` (``statistic``, ``df``, ``p_value``; ``None`` unless both ``"fe"`` and ``"re"`` are estimated and the test can be computed) and ``.interpret()``.  The Hausman test compares the fixed- and random-effects coefficients of the regressors with classical covariance matrices that share the residual variance of the fixed-effects model, so that their difference is positive semi-definite.  Needs the ``linearmodels`` library (the ``[panel]`` extra). |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q "geo4bolivia[panel]"
import geo4bolivia as geo

panel = geo.combine(["ntl", "pop"], scale="municipality")
res = geo.panel_model(panel, "ln_NTLpc", "pop_density")
print(res.interpret())
res.gt
```

